	'tb_title'           => 'The Build',
	'tb_sub'             => 'Five steps · forty turns',
	'tb_intro'           => 'Every drink at Yoru Bar follows five repeatable, disciplined steps. We rely on clear ice, precise measurements, and thirty seconds of patient stirring.',
	'tb_s0h'             => 'The Five Steps of the Build',
	'tb_s0b'             => 'Every cocktail at our counter follows five unhurried steps. First, step 01 begins with dense, crystal-clear ice cut every Tuesday from a 25 kg block into precise 5 cm cubes. In step 02, each spirit and modifier is measured strictly by jigger to ensure balance and consistency. For step 03, the drink is stirred forty turns—about thirty seconds—with a long barspoon, achieving optimal chilling without over-dilution. Step 04 strains the cold liquid into a coupe kept ready at −18 °C. Finally, step 05 expresses the citrus peel over the glass to release essential oils, after which the peel is discarded.',
	'tb_s1h'             => 'Forty Turns by Hand',
	'tb_s1b'             => 'Behind the counter, Kofi Mensah and Theo Lambrecht oversee afternoon prep long before our first guests descend the basement steps. We do not shake cocktails that call for clarity, nor do we rush dilution with chipped ice. Forty deliberate turns of the barspoon create a smooth, dense mouthfeel, allowing vermouth and spirits to integrate evenly. The tactile feedback through the spoon tells the bartender when the temperature has dropped to its ideal point. By pouring into glassware chilled to −18 °C, that crisp temperature is preserved throughout your first sips.',
	'tb_s2h'             => 'Why do you cut ice from a 25 kg block?',
	'tb_s2b'             => 'Ice is an essential ingredient in every stirred drink, dictating both temperature and water content. Every Tuesday morning, Theo receives a clear 25 kg block of directional-frozen ice on our prep bench. Using a serrated knife and hand saw, he saws the block into uniform 5 cm cubes. Because dense, directional-frozen ice contains no trapped air or impurities, it melts much slower than standard machine ice. This deliberate cube keeps a cocktail cold without breaking down prematurely, ensuring the final sip remains as balanced as the first.',
	'os_title'           => 'On Service',
